win32u: set the queue masks after handling internal hardware messages

process_driver_events() stores the caller's wake and changed masks on the
server and then handles pending internal hardware messages. A handler can send
a message to another thread; wait_message_reply() then replaces the masks with
QS_SMRESULT and nothing restores them. The caller goes on to wait on a queue
that no posted or sent message can signal, until the next internal message
happens to arrive.
Power BI Desktop reaches this through the cursor resource patch. The server
addresses WM_WINE_SETCURSOR to the top-level window, so Desktop's UI thread
handles the cursors of the embedded browser. The Wayland driver asks for the
cursor image, and a cursor owned by the browser process is resolved with a
blocking send to its owner, 50 ms at most. A cursor that is not a shared
system cursor (column resize, grab) is never cached and is sent every time.
When the owner did not answer in time, the UI thread stopped answering: calls
from the report page to the host stalled and Desktop appeared frozen until the
pointer shape changed again. Whether a promptly answered lookup can do the
same was not determined.
Observed on 2026-11-18 for 6 min 48 s: the UI thread asleep in
NtUserWaitMessage, server queue masks 0x8000/0x8000, wake bits 0x158, two sent
and four posted messages pending. Moving the pointer onto a window of that
thread woke it. tests/e2e/cursor-wake.py stops the owner for 400 ms during a
lookup: the UI thread stopped answering in every round before this change and
in none after it.
Handle the internal messages first, then set the masks. Wine master had the
same order as 11.18 on that date.
Wine-derived change: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

--- a/dlls/win32u/message.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/message.c
@@ -3349,6 +3349,16 @@
 
     if (check_internal_bits( QS_DRIVER )) drained = user_driver->pProcessEvents( events_mask );
 
+    /* Process every pending internal hardware message before setting the queue
+     * masks: a handler can send a message to another thread, and waiting for
+     * that reply replaces the masks the caller is about to sleep on. */
+    if (check_internal_bits( QS_HARDWARE ))
+    {
+        struct peek_message_filter filter = {.internal = TRUE};
+        MSG msg;
+        peek_message( &msg, &filter );
+    }
+
     if (drained || !check_queue_masks( wake_mask, changed_mask ))
     {
         SERVER_START_REQ( set_queue_mask )
@@ -3361,14 +3371,6 @@
         SERVER_END_REQ;
     }
 
-    /* process every pending internal hardware messages */
-    if (check_internal_bits( QS_HARDWARE ))
-    {
-        struct peek_message_filter filter = {.internal = TRUE};
-        MSG msg;
-        peek_message( &msg, &filter );
-    }
-
     return is_queue_signaled();
 }
 
